// REJECTS: Unexpected token 'COLUMN_LEVEL'. Valid alternatives: ['ROW_LEVEL', 'AGGREGATE']
//
// `type:` on a relation validation is a CLOSED two-value token set, not an identifier that
// is mapped to an enum later. DataQualityLexerGrammar declares ROW_LEVEL and AGGREGATE as
// their own tokens and `validationType` names exactly those two, so an unknown value dies in
// the parser with the full alternative list rather than in a downstream valueOf().
//
// Worth pinning because the sibling grammars in this batch do the opposite and disagree with
// each other: Snowflake's `permissionScheme` is an identifier validated by the walker (see
// ./neg-snowflake-permission-scheme.pure) and Snowflake's `accountType` is an identifier that
// is NOT validated at all. Three enum-shaped fields, three different enforcement points. A
// rewrite that picks one policy for all of them diverges on two thirds of them.
//
// `type` is placed before `assertion` here on purpose: after a lambda the parser's error
// recovery reports the colon instead, which names nothing.
###DataQualityValidation
DataQualityRelationValidation fixture::neg::UnknownValidationType
{
   query: |#>{fixture::neg::Store.myTable}#->from(fixture::neg::Runtime);
   validations: [
   {
     name: 'nameIsNotError';
     type: COLUMN_LEVEL;
     assertion: row|$row.name != 'error';
   }
   ];
}
